WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.792
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Audit
logs in Google Cloud

00:00:01.792 --> 00:00:03.300
provide an immutable
record of how

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.880
resources and data are created,
modified, and accessed.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:08.500
But knowing what's
liked by default

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.170
and what you have control
over can be confusing.

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:13.200
Let's walk through setting
up and reading audit

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.660
logs to understand what's
really going on in your project.

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.100
This is the "Stack Doctor,"

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.908
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.940
Actions that touch Google
Cloud resources or data

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.460
within those resources
create an audit trail.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.138
But where do you go
to see that trail?

00:00:29.138 --> 00:00:31.180
You can start on the
Activity tab of the homepage

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.299
of your project.

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:34.955
This is a view of all of the
audited events in your project,

00:00:34.955 --> 00:00:36.580
and you get more
information about what

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:38.270
happened for each event.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.770
Let's look at a
specific example.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:42.580
We'll use Cloud storage and look
at both out-of-the-box audit

00:00:42.580 --> 00:00:45.310
logs and those you
can enable yourself.

00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.560
Start by creating a Cloud
Storage Bucket with a name

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.970
and default options.

00:00:48.970 --> 00:00:51.160
Feel free to delete
this after you're done.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:53.775
Now switch back to the
Activity tab and refresh.

00:00:53.775 --> 00:00:55.150
Once the bucket
has been created,

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:57.970
you'll see a new event showing
the resource that was created,

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:01.190
the user who created it, and
the timestamp of the event.

00:01:01.190 --> 00:01:03.700
You can also go to
Logging in the GCP console

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.620
and see the matching log entry.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:08.780
It's an activity log
and the type audit log.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.150
That's pretty straightforward.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.240
You created a resource and
a log entry is written.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.860
However, if you try adding
a file to your new bucket,

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.250
you'll see that no additional
activity or log entries

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:20.080
are generated as
a result. This is

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.000
because operating on
objects in buckets

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.860
is interacting
with user data, not

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.560
creating or modifying
the resources themselves.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.440
To lock these actions, we'll
need to enable additional audit

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:30.190
logging.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.110
You can find the Audit
Logging Configuration

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.063
under the IM an Admin
section of the Products menu.

00:01:35.063 --> 00:01:36.730
This screen shows you
the kinds of audit

00:01:36.730 --> 00:01:39.053
logs you can enable
for GCP services.

00:01:39.053 --> 00:01:41.470
You should know that System
Event and Admin Activity audit

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:44.510
logs are always enabled
and cannot be turned off.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.750
There are three kinds of
data access audit logs

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:48.130
you can enable.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.560
Admin Read Logs are written
when you access Resource

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.630
Configuration or Metadata.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:55.390
Data Read Logs are written when
user data within a resource

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:56.560
is accessed.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.530
And Data Write Logs are written
when data within a resource

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:00.550
is created.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.510
Let's continue with
our example to clarify.

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.330
If you enable Admin
to Read Audit Logs

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.620
and refresh the bucket
view in the console,

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:08.342
you'll see that a
new event is written.

00:02:08.342 --> 00:02:10.259
This is a record of you
accessing the resource

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.280
as a metadata and configuration.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.980
The actual log entry will show
a call to the storage buckets

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.280
GET API.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.490
If you enable Data Read Audit
Logs and refresh the bucket,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:22.390
you'll see an event written
for accessing resource data,

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.400
logging your call to the
storage object's List API.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.950
If you then retrieve a file
from your storage bucket,

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.640
you'll see another event
created, showing you accessing

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.160
the resources data.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.770
The log entry shows a call to
the storage object's GET API.

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.230
Finally, if you enable
Data Write Audit Logs

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.400
and create a new
file in the bucket,

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:42.520
you'll see an event for
inserting data, logging

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:45.550
a call to the storage
object's Create API.

00:02:45.550 --> 00:02:46.850
That was a lot.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.090
Let's review the kinds of
audit logs you can enable

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:51.220
and what actions they
can actually record.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.500
You can turn on three
kinds of audit logs,

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.500
Admin Read for reading
metadata and configurations,

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.710
Data Read for reading
user data, and Data Write

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.860
for creating or writing
user data within resources.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:04.315
Because these are
user-controlled,

00:03:04.315 --> 00:03:06.190
they do incur logging
charges in the same way

00:03:06.190 --> 00:03:07.930
as other logs in your project.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.990
System Event and Admin Activity
logs are always written,

00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:13.000
cannot be disabled,
and therefore,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.700
don't incur logging charges.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.180
Audit logging is
invaluable as you

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.147
work to keep your deployments
in GCP secure and compliant.

00:03:19.147 --> 00:03:21.730
Knowing how to use Audit Logging
to get a clear record of what

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:24.070
happened in the project
is essential to keeping

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.700
a Google Cloud project secure.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:27.825
And I hope that this helps
you to better understand

00:03:27.825 --> 00:03:28.640
how to do that.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:31.140
Thank you for joining me today,
and come back soon for more.

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:33.790
This is the Stack Doctor,
stay healthy out there.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:37.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

